          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line drug therapy for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "**The retrieved passages don't clearly name the first-line drug for type 2 diabetes.** I could not confirm an answer from the sources I can access, so I won't name one from memory.\n\n**What the passages do say** (from the *Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management* guidance, National diabetes association standards of care, educational summary):\n\n- That guidance refers to metformin as the baseline agent. It describes metformin's kidney-function dosing limits and the need to check vitamin B12 with long-term use. It also says agents with proven heart, kidney or heart-failure benefit are preferred for people who have those conditions, \"whether or not metformin is also used.\"\n- The sentence that explicitly names the first-line choice was not among the passages returned, so I can't quote it. My search for it failed when the tool-call limit was reached.\n\n**Disagreements:** The deep research search returned no disagreement section for type 2 diabetes drug choice. The disagreements it did return concern hypertension, urinary tract infection and lipid treatment, which are outside this question, so I have not used them.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Check the opening section of the diabetes guidance document, or the primary guideline it summarizes, for the explicit first-line statement.\n- Ask a clinician which medicine fits a specific situation. Choice depends on kidney function, heart and kidney history, and other health factors.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
